many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism the question is  which book should I read first and it's  specifically about stoic literature this  came up in Facebook but it's a great  question about philosophy in general  it's a common concern that many people  have they want to begin with the right  book or the best book or the book that's  going to lay down the proper foundation  for all of their future studies and I  have a few things to say about this the  shortest answer to this question is that  there isn't any book that you should  start with there is no right book there  is no best book there is no  one-size-fits-all piece of literature  that you should begin your studies of  Stoicism with now if I were a beginner  wanting to get into stoicism and I asked  that question and I got that answer I  would say wait a second dr. Sandler that  is profoundly unhelpful what are you  talking about so let me give a longer  explanation that's gonna range over a  few points let's begin by saying that in  a way this frees you up you can read  whatever book is going to be helpful for  you I strongly suggest that at some  point in time you're going to want to  actually read the classic stoic text and  by that I mean epictetus Seneca Marcus  Aurelius but you can begin if it works  for you with some of the more popular  stoic literature that's out there but  you know understand that you're you're  getting somebody else's interpretation  rather than the original source material  and that's okay now why should I as a  scholar researcher on stoicism adopt  such a Leslie ferrule might even say  lackadaisical attitude towards what you  ought to read it's not just because it  has to do with you  as an individual reader who can make  their own decisions about what they want  to read it's also because I think it's  good to get out of the way this notion  that by reading the right text at the  beginning you're going to save yourself  a lot of trouble or set down the right  foundation or at least not get things  wrong about stoicism that's not really  the case as a matter of fact when it  comes to philosophical literature you  should expect that the first time that  you read something you're probably not  even going to get half of what's going  on in that book and you're probably  gonna have to go back to it and you'll  miss remember things and that's  perfectly fine that's normal that's the  way it works that happens even with me  today you know rereading  Epictetus after 25 years maybe even more  of reading him I still find stuff in  there that I missed the last time around  and that is okay so if that happens to  me it's gonna happen to you what you  should expect is whatever book you pick  you're gonna read that and it may be  very helpful for you and then you're  gonna read some other stuff and that's  gonna tell you more about stoicism then  you're gonna come back to that first  book and read it again and you're going  to get more out of it the second time  and instead of like laying down a solid  foundation that you're going to be  building on think of it more as an  iterative process where you're you're  getting closer and closer and closer to  understanding what it is that these  authors are trying to communicate to you  this iterative ongoing process of  reading and really discovery is  something that is even more applicable  to practical philosophy which is what  stoicism is it's a philosophy of life so  you can begin with Epictetus is  enchiridion and then you know read a  popular book or read you know another  classic stoic texts like maybe Senecas  on anger and then come back to epictetus  and you're going to find that you  understand more that you've acquired  something in the process you might go  from epictetus is enchiridion into his  discourses or you might read one of the  great secondary works on Epictetus or on  stoicism in general all of that is  perfectly fine it doesn't matter so much  what book you start with what matters is  whether you're making a sort of ongoing  commitment to studying stoicism because  you can't get a full perspective on  stoicism from any of the secondary  literature works out there let alone the  popular ones and you also can't get it  just from epictetus or just from Seneca  or just from marcus aurelius you if you  really want to understand stoicism  you've got to do some reading around and  you've got to do some rereading and  that's great because again the more  times you do that the more you will be  rewarded with developing a better and  better understanding so hopefully  hearing that answer you feel kind of a  sense of relief and and liberation  rather than just confusion and wishing  that I'd said one book in particular if  you found this answer helpful for you  then please share it with others who  might benefit from it this work is  entirely supported by crowdfunding on my  patreon site so if you find it valuable  consider becoming a supporter at  patreon.com slash Saddler
 

